meta {
  name: health
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/internal/rcs/health
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  Whether the paired phone is currently able to send.

  The service talks to the phone itself — there is no separate sender process.
  It holds a Google Messages web session for a phone paired through
  `Pairing / page`, and that session lives in the `rcs_sessions` table so it
  survives restarts.

  ```
  curl http://localhost:8080/internal/rcs/health
  ```

  ## Reading the response

  ```json
  {
    "success": true,
    "data": {
      "ready": true,
      "sender": {
        "loggedIn": true,
        "connected": true,
        "phoneResponding": true,
        "sessionActive": true,
        "sims": [{ "carrier": "AT&T", "number": "...", "rcsEnabled": true }],
        "lastError": ""
      }
    }
  }
  ```

  `ready` is the only field worth gating on. It is true when every one of
  `loggedIn`, `connected`, `phoneResponding`, `sessionActive` holds **and** at
  least one SIM has `rcsEnabled`. Anything else means sends will be refused.

  What each false value usually means:

  - `loggedIn` false — no phone is paired at all, or the pairing was revoked
  - `connected` false — check `lastError`; an HTTP 401 there means the Google
    session expired and needs `Pairing / refresh with HAR`
  - `sessionActive` false — somebody opened messages.google.com/web on the same
    Google account and took the session
  - `phoneResponding` false — the handset is off, offline or asleep
  - no SIM with `rcsEnabled` — RCS / chat features were turned off on the phone

  The health monitor polls this internally and logs (or alerts, if an announcer
  is wired in) when it flips, once per transition rather than once per poll. You do not
  need to watch this endpoint yourself.

  Errors:
  - 503 — `rcs.enabled` is false in config, so the subsystem is off entirely
}
